\section{Details}{

\subsection{Obtaining an object}{

\code{GDALMultiDimRaster$openMDArray(path)}\cr
Opens the array at \code{path}, a character string giving the full path of the
array from the root group, e.g., \code{"/temperature"} or
\code{"/group/temperature"}. Returns an object of class \code{GDALMDArray}. An error
is raised if no array is found at \code{path}.
}

\subsection{Read/write fields}{

\code{$readByteAsRaw}\cr
A logical value, \code{TRUE} to read arrays of data type Byte as \R \code{raw}
vectors. Defaults to \code{FALSE} (Byte is read as \code{integer}).

\code{$quiet}\cr
A logical value, \code{TRUE} to suppress console output from class methods.
Defaults to \code{FALSE} (e.g., a progress bar from
\code{$computeStatistics()}).
}

\subsection{Methods}{

\code{$isOpen()}\cr
Returns logical \code{TRUE} if the array is open, otherwise \code{FALSE}.

\code{$getName()}\cr
Returns the name of the array.

\code{$getFullName()}\cr
Returns the full path of the array.

\code{$getDimensionCount()}\cr
Returns the number of dimensions of the array (\code{0} for a scalar array).

\code{$getDimensions()}\cr
Returns a data frame describing the dimensions of the array in its own
order, with columns \code{name}, \code{full_name}, \code{size}, \code{type}, \code{direction} and
\code{indexing_variable}, as described for
\code{GDALMultiDimRaster$getDimensions()}.

\code{$getDataType()}\cr
Returns a list describing the data type of the array, with elements
\code{class} (\code{"NUMERIC"}, \code{"STRING"} or \code{"COMPOUND"}), \code{numeric_type} (the
GDAL data type name such as \code{"Int16"} or \code{"Float32"}, or \code{""} if not
numeric), \code{name} (the data type name, used for compound types) and \code{size}
(in bytes).

\code{$getAttributes()}\cr
Returns a named list of the attributes of the array, converted as described
for \code{GDALMultiDimRaster$getAttributes()}.

\code{$getNoDataValue()}\cr
Returns the nodata value of the array as a numeric value, or \code{NA} if no
nodata value is set.

\code{$getScale()}\cr
Returns the scale value of the array (e.g., from the \code{scale_factor}
attribute in netCDF), or \code{NA} if not set. Values returned by
\code{$read()} are not scaled. The unscaled value is
\code{raw_value * scale + offset}.

\code{$getOffset()}\cr
Returns the offset value of the array (e.g., from the \code{add_offset}
attribute in netCDF), or \code{NA} if not set.

\code{$getUnit()}\cr
Returns the unit of the array values as a character string (e.g., \code{"K"},
\code{"degC"}, or \code{"hours since 1900-01-01"} for a time coordinate), or \code{""} if
not set.

\code{$getSpatialRef()}\cr
Returns the spatial reference system of the array as a WKT string, or \code{""}
if not known.

\code{$getBlockSize()}\cr
Returns a numeric vector with the block (chunk) size of the array along
each dimension. A value of \code{0} means that no block size is known for that
dimension. Reading whole blocks is the most efficient access pattern for
chunked formats such as Zarr, netCDF-4 and HDF5.

\code{$getInfo()}\cr
Returns a named list with elements \code{name}, \code{full_name}, \code{dimensions},
\code{datatype}, \code{block_size}, \code{nodata}, \code{scale}, \code{offset}, \code{unit}, \code{srs} and
\code{attributes}, combining the methods above in one call.

\code{$getCoordinateValues(dim)}\cr
Returns the values of the indexing variable (coordinate array) of the
dimension at 0-based index \code{dim}, or \code{NULL} if the dimension has no
indexing variable.

\code{$read(start, count, step)}\cr
Reads the whole array, or a hyperslab of it. \code{start} is a numeric vector of
0-based start indexes and \code{count} a numeric vector of the number of values
to read, each with one value per dimension of the array. \code{step} is a
numeric vector giving the step between indexes for each dimension (e.g.,
\code{2} to read every second value, or a negative value to read in reverse
order), or \code{NULL} for a step of \code{1}. Use \code{start = NULL} and \code{count = NULL}
to read the whole array, i.e., \code{$read(NULL, NULL, NULL)}. All three
arguments are required (see Description), but \code{\link[=mdim_read]{mdim_read()}} provides
defaults for them.

The value returned by \code{$read()} is a plain vector, with the last
dimension varying fastest (C order, the same layout as data returned by
\code{GDALRaster$read()}), and with no \R attributes. See \code{\link[=mdim_read]{mdim_read()}} for a
reader that also returns dimension and coordinate information. Data types
follow \code{GDALRaster$read()}: integer types that fit in 32 bits are returned
as \code{integer} (or Byte as \code{raw} if \code{$readByteAsRaw} is \code{TRUE}), other
numeric types as \code{double} (64-bit integer values are exact up to 2^53),
complex types as \code{complex}, and string arrays as \code{character}. The nodata
value is returned as \code{NA}, as is \code{NaN} for floating point types. Arrays of
compound data type are not supported.
}

\subsection{Derived arrays}{

The methods below return a new object of class \code{GDALMDArray} that is a
view of this array. No data are read or copied until the new array is
read. The new array holds its own reference to the dataset, so it remains
valid after this array or the dataset is closed. Its fields
(\code{$readByteAsRaw}, \code{$quiet}) start with the values of this
array.

\code{$getView(view_expr)}\cr
Returns a view of the array given a character string \code{view_expr}, using
the same syntax as NumPy basic slicing and indexing (0-based), or field
access by name, as described in section "View Expressions" of
\code{\link[=mdim_as_classic]{mdim_as_classic()}}. For example, \code{"[0,...]"} selects the first index of
the first dimension (removing that dimension), \code{"[1:3,:,::2]"} takes
indexes 1 and 2 of the first dimension and every second index of the
third, and \code{"[::-1,...]"} reverses the first dimension. Note that GDAL
renames the sliced dimensions of a view (e.g., \code{"subset_lat_2_1_2"}), and
they then have no indexing variable.

\code{$transpose(axes)}\cr
Returns a view of the array with its dimensions reordered. \code{axes} is an
integer vector of 0-based dimension indexes of this array, giving for each
dimension of the new array the dimension of this array that it comes from.
For example, for an array with dimensions \verb{time, lat, lon}, \code{axes = c(2, 1, 0)} gives an array with dimensions \verb{lon, lat, time}. A value of \code{-1}
inserts a new dimension of size 1.

\code{$getUnscaled()}\cr
Returns a view of the array with the scale and offset applied
(\code{raw_value * scale + offset}). The new array has data type \code{Float64} (or
\code{CFloat64} for a complex array), no scale or offset, and the nodata value
\code{NaN} if this array has a nodata value (read as \code{NA}).

\code{$getMask(options)}\cr
Returns a view of the array of data type Byte, with the value \code{1} where a
value of this array is valid and \code{0} where it is not, as determined from
the nodata value, and the attributes \verb{_FillValue}, \code{missing_value},
\code{valid_min}, \code{valid_max} and \code{valid_range}, if present (and \code{NaN} for
floating point types). \code{options} is a character vector of \code{NAME=VALUE}
pairs, or \code{NULL} (see \code{GDALMDArray::GetMask()} in the GDAL API
documentation).

\code{$getResampled(new_dim_sizes, resample_alg, target_srs, options)}\cr
Returns a view of the array resampled to new dimension sizes, and
optionally reprojected, along its horizontal dimensions (types
\code{"HORIZONTAL_X"} and \code{"HORIZONTAL_Y"}). Values are computed when the new
array is read, using GDAL's warping engine, so reading a window of the new
array reads only the part of the source array that it needs.
Requires GDAL >= 3.4.
\itemize{
\item \code{new_dim_sizes}: a numeric vector with one value per dimension giving
the new size, or \code{NA} to keep a dimension unchanged (or, for the
horizontal dimensions, to have GDAL compute the size). \code{NULL} is the same
as all \code{NA}. Dimensions that are not horizontal must be \code{NA}. The new
horizontal dimensions are evenly spaced over the extent of the source.
\item \code{resample_alg}: a character string, one of \code{"nearest"}, \code{"bilinear"},
\code{"cubic"}, \code{"cubicspline"}, \code{"lanczos"}, \code{"average"}, \code{"rms"} or
\code{"mode"}.
\item \code{target_srs}: a character string giving the spatial reference system to
reproject to, in a format accepted by \code{\link[=srs_to_wkt]{srs_to_wkt()}} (e.g.,
\code{"EPSG:3031"}), or \code{""} to keep the spatial reference of the array. An
error is raised if \code{target_srs} is given and the array has no spatial
reference (a multidimensional VRT can add one, see the examples).
\item \code{options}: a character vector of \code{NAME=VALUE} pairs, or \code{NULL} (see
\code{GDALMDArray::GetResampled()} in the GDAL API documentation).
}

The new horizontal dimensions are named \code{dimX} and \code{dimY}, with indexing
variables of the same names holding the coordinates of the cell centres
in the target spatial reference.

\code{$getGridded(grid_options, x_array, y_array, options)}\cr
Returns a gridded view of an array of scattered points, i.e., an array
whose last dimension indexes the points, interpolated onto a regular grid
using the GDAL gridding algorithms (as for \command{gdal_grid}).
Requires GDAL >= 3.7.
\itemize{
\item \code{grid_options}: a character string giving the gridding algorithm and its
parameters, as for the \command{gdal_grid} \code{-a} argument, e.g.,
\code{"invdist:power=2.0:smoothing=1.0"}, \code{"nearest"}, \code{"average"} or
\code{"linear"}.
\item \code{x_array}, \code{y_array}: objects of class \code{GDALMDArray} with the X and Y
coordinates of the points (one dimension, the same as the last dimension
of this array), or \code{NULL} for both to use the arrays named in the
\code{coordinates} attribute of this array.
\item \code{options}: a character vector of \code{NAME=VALUE} pairs, or \code{NULL}, e.g.,
\code{"RESOLUTION=0.5"} for the grid spacing, and \code{"XMIN=..."}, \code{"XMAX=..."},
\code{"YMIN=..."}, \code{"YMAX=..."} for its extent (see \code{GDALMDArray::GetGridded()}
in the GDAL API documentation).
}

The new dimensions are named \code{dimX} and \code{dimY}, with indexing variables of
the same names.
}

\subsection{Chunk-aware I/O and statistics}{

\code{$adviseRead(start, count, options)}\cr
Advises the driver of an upcoming read of the hyperslab given by \code{start}
and \code{count} (as for \code{$read()}, both \code{NULL} for the whole array).
Many drivers ignore the advice, but it can greatly accelerate access with
some. The Zarr driver fetches and decodes the blocks that intersect the
hyperslab using multiple threads and keeps them in memory for the reads
that follow on this array object (the call returns when that is done).
\code{options} is a character vector of driver-specific \code{NAME=VALUE} pairs, or
\code{NULL} (requires GDAL >= 3.4 when not empty). For Zarr these are
\code{"CACHE_SIZE=<bytes>"}, the maximum memory to use (by default half of the
remaining GDAL block cache), and \code{"NUM_THREADS=<integer>|ALL_CPUS"} (by
default from the \code{GDAL_NUM_THREADS} configuration option). Returns logical
\code{TRUE} on success (ignoring the advice is a success).

\code{$getProcessingChunkSize(max_chunk_memory)}\cr
Returns a numeric vector with a chunk size along each dimension, suitable
for processing the array piece by piece. The chunk is a multiple of the
block size (see \code{$getBlockSize()}) along each dimension, within
\code{max_chunk_memory} bytes, or one block if a block is larger than that. For
an array with no block size, the chunk is built up from the last
dimension. See \code{\link[=mdim_chunk_index]{mdim_chunk_index()}} for the index of chunks of a given
size.

\code{$getStatistics(approx_ok, force)}\cr
Returns a named numeric vector of statistics of the array values: \code{min},
\code{max}, \code{mean}, \code{sd} (standard deviation) and \code{valid_count} (the number of
values that are not nodata). Statistics already stored for the array are
returned if there are any. Otherwise, they are computed if \code{force} is
\code{TRUE}, or \code{NA} values are returned if \code{force} is \code{FALSE}. \code{approx_ok} is
a logical value, \code{TRUE} to allow statistics computed from a subset of the
values (currently ignored by GDAL, which always uses all of the values).
Statistics are of the values as stored (scale and offset not applied).

\code{$computeStatistics(approx_ok, options)}\cr
Computes statistics of the array values and returns them as a named numeric
vector, as for \code{$getStatistics()}. The array is read one processing
chunk at a time, so memory use is bounded for arrays of any size. A
progress bar is shown unless \code{$quiet} is \code{TRUE}. \code{options} is a
character vector of \code{NAME=VALUE} pairs, or \code{NULL} (requires GDAL >= 3.8
when not empty, e.g., \code{"UPDATE_METADATA=YES"} to store the minimum and
maximum in the \code{actual_range} attribute of a netCDF or Zarr array opened
for update). Once computed, the statistics are generally saved by GDAL
for later calls to \code{$getStatistics()}, in a file \code{pam.aux.xml} or
\verb{<filename>.aux.xml} next to the dataset, written when the dataset is
closed. If that location is not writable (e.g., a remote dataset), GDAL
reports a failure to write the file at that time, which can be ignored.

\code{$cache(options)}\cr
Caches the content of the array in a file named as the dataset filename
with the extension \code{.gmac} added (a netCDF dataset), or in the directory
given by the \code{GDAL_PAM_PROXY_DIR} configuration option if that file cannot
be written. Reads of the array then use the cache automatically, also in
later sessions. The main purpose is to cache a view that is expensive to
compute, such as a transposed array. The cache is not checked against the
source: it must be deleted manually if the source array changes. \code{options}
is a character vector of \code{NAME=VALUE} pairs, or \code{NULL}. Currently the only
option is \code{"BLOCKSIZE=bs0,bs1,...,bsN"}, the block size of the cached
array. Returns logical \code{TRUE} on success. Requires GDAL >= 3.4.
}

\subsection{Raw block references}{

\code{$getRawBlockInfo(block_coords)}\cr
Returns the location of one block (chunk) of the array as stored, without
reading or decoding it. \code{block_coords} is a numeric vector of 0-based
block coordinates, one value per dimension, i.e., the index of the block
along each dimension (columns \code{chunkoff} of \code{\link[=mdim_chunk_index]{mdim_chunk_index()}}). For a
dimension with a block size of \code{0} the only valid coordinate is \code{0}.
Returns a named list with elements:
\itemize{
\item \code{filename}: the file that contains the block (may be a \verb{/vsi} path or,
for virtual stores such as kerchunk or Icechunk references, the file the
reference points to), or \code{NA}
\item \code{offset}: the offset in bytes of the start of the block in that file
(numeric, exact up to 2^53), or \code{NA}
\item \code{size}: the size in bytes of the block as stored (i.e., compressed if
compression is used), or \code{NA}
\item \code{info}: character vector of driver-specific \code{NAME=VALUE} pairs that
describe how the block is encoded
\item \code{inline_data}: a \code{raw} vector with the content of the block if the
dataset holds it inline and not in a file (in which case \code{filename} and
\code{offset} are \code{NA}), otherwise \code{NULL}
}

A block that has valid coordinates but is missing in the dataset (all
values are nodata) has \code{NA} for \code{filename}, \code{offset} and \code{size}. The
\code{info} values depend on the format. For multi-byte data types they should
include \code{ENDIANNESS} (\code{LITTLE} or \code{BIG}). For netCDF-4 and HDF5 they may
include \code{COMPRESSION} (\code{DEFLATE} or \code{SZIP}) and \code{FILTER} (e.g.,
\code{SHUFFLE}). For Zarr they may include \code{COMPRESSOR} and \code{FILTERS} (JSON
as in the array metadata) and \code{TRANSPOSE_ORDER}. An error is raised if
the block coordinates are out of range, or if the format driver does not
provide this information for the array (it is available for netCDF-4,
HDF5, Zarr and, under conditions, multidimensional VRT). Requires GDAL >=
3.12. For netCDF-4, GDAL must also have the HDF5 driver.

\code{$getRawBlockRefs(block_coords)}\cr
Returns the locations of a set of blocks as a data frame, with one row per
block. \code{block_coords} is a numeric matrix of 0-based block coordinates
with one row per block and one column per dimension (e.g., the \code{chunkoff}
columns of a matrix from \code{\link[=mdim_chunk_index]{mdim_chunk_index()}}). The data frame has columns
\code{filename}, \code{offset}, \code{size}, \code{info} and \code{inline_data}, as described for
\code{$getRawBlockInfo()}, of which \code{info} and \code{inline_data} are list
columns. Rows are in the order of \code{block_coords}, with \code{NA} values for
missing blocks. A progress bar is shown for more than one block unless
\code{$quiet} is \code{TRUE}. For Zarr, each block is a file (or a part of a
file) that GDAL has to query, so for a remote store this takes one or
more network requests per block. See \code{\link[=mdim_block_refs]{mdim_block_refs()}}, which returns
the references along with the chunk index for the whole array or for a
hyperslab. Requires GDAL >= 3.12.
}

\subsection{Classic raster view}{

\code{$asClassicDataset(idx_xdim, idx_ydim, options)}\cr
Returns a view of the array as a classic (2D) raster, an object of class
\code{\link{GDALRaster}}. \code{idx_xdim} and \code{idx_ydim} are the 0-based
indexes of the dimensions to use as the X (width) and Y (height) axes.
Any other dimensions become raster bands. \code{idx_ydim} is ignored for a 1D
array. \code{options} is a character vector of \code{NAME=VALUE} pairs, or \code{NULL}
(see \code{GDALMDArray::AsClassicDataset()} in the GDAL API documentation; it
requires GDAL >= 3.8 when not empty). The geotransform is taken from
regularly spaced indexing variables of the X and Y dimensions. No data are
copied: the raster reads from the array, so a view from
\code{$getView()} can be used to take, e.g., one time step of a 4D array
as a \code{GDALRaster}, which can be passed to any function that works on a
\code{GDALRaster} (e.g., \code{\link[=warp]{warp()}}, \code{\link[=plot_raster]{plot_raster()}}). The \code{GDALRaster} remains
valid after the array is closed. See also \code{\link[=mdim_as_classic]{mdim_as_classic()}}, which opens
a dataset and returns the same raster in one call.

\code{$close()}\cr
Closes the array and releases the reference to its dataset.
}
}

\examples{
\dontshow{if (gdal_version_num() >= gdal_compute_version(3, 2, 0) && isTRUE(gdal_formats("netCDF")$multidim_raster)) withAutoprint(\{ # examplesIf}
f <- system.file("extdata/mdim_small.nc", package="gdalraster")
ds <- new(GDALMultiDimRaster, f)
arr <- ds$openMDArray("/t2m")
arr

arr$getDimensions()
arr$getDataType()
arr$getBlockSize()
c(arr$getScale(), arr$getOffset())
arr$getUnit()

# coordinate values of the first dimension (time)
arr$getCoordinateValues(0)

# the first time step, as stored (not scaled)
v <- arr$read(start = c(0, 0, 0), count = c(1, 5, 6), step = NULL)
v

# one grid cell, all time steps, scaled to degC
arr$read(c(0, 2, 3), c(4, 1, 1), NULL) * arr$getScale() + arr$getOffset()

# the same, from a view with the scale and offset applied
arr$getUnscaled()$read(c(0, 2, 3), c(4, 1, 1), NULL)

# a chunk size for processing within 120 bytes (60 values of Int16)
arr$getProcessingChunkSize(120)

# the first time step as a view, and its mask of valid values
v1 <- arr$getView("[0,...]")
v1$getDimensions()$size
v1$getMask(NULL)$read(NULL, NULL, NULL)

# lon, lat, time order
arr$transpose(c(2, 1, 0))$getDimensions()$name

# the first time step as a classic raster (lon is X, lat is Y)
r <- v1$asClassicDataset(1, 0, NULL)
r
r$getGeoTransform()
r$close()

arr$close()
ds$close()

## statistics of the stored values, read one processing chunk at a time
# (on a copy of the file, since GDAL saves the statistics next to it)
f_tmp <- file.path(tempdir(), "mdim_small.nc")
file.copy(f, f_tmp)
ds <- new(GDALMultiDimRaster, f_tmp)
arr <- ds$openMDArray("/t2m")
arr$quiet <- TRUE
arr$computeStatistics(FALSE, NULL)
arr$close()
ds$close()
\dontshow{unlink(c(f_tmp, paste0(f_tmp, ".aux.xml")))}

## resampling and reprojection (GDAL >= 3.4)
if (gdal_version_num() >= gdal_compute_version(3, 4, 0)) {
  # t2m has no spatial reference: add one with a multidimensional VRT,
  # which reads from the source file (nothing is copied)
  vrt <- '<VRTDataset><Group name="/">
    <Dimension name="time" size="4" indexingVariable="time"/>
    <Dimension name="lat" type="HORIZONTAL_Y" size="5" indexingVariable="lat"/>
    <Dimension name="lon" type="HORIZONTAL_X" size="6" indexingVariable="lon"/>
    <Array name="time"><DataType>Float64</DataType><DimensionRef ref="time"/>
      <Source><SourceFilename>SRC</SourceFilename>
      <SourceArray>/time</SourceArray></Source></Array>
    <Array name="lat"><DataType>Float64</DataType><DimensionRef ref="lat"/>
      <Source><SourceFilename>SRC</SourceFilename>
      <SourceArray>/lat</SourceArray></Source></Array>
    <Array name="lon"><DataType>Float64</DataType><DimensionRef ref="lon"/>
      <Source><SourceFilename>SRC</SourceFilename>
      <SourceArray>/lon</SourceArray></Source></Array>
    <Array name="t2m"><DataType>Int16</DataType>
      <DimensionRef ref="time"/><DimensionRef ref="lat"/>
      <DimensionRef ref="lon"/>
      <SRS dataAxisToSRSAxisMapping="2,1">EPSG:4326</SRS>
      <NoDataValue>-32767</NoDataValue>
      <Source><SourceFilename>SRC</SourceFilename>
      <SourceArray>/t2m</SourceArray></Source></Array>
  </Group></VRTDataset>'
  vrt <- gsub("SRC", f, vrt, fixed = TRUE)

  ds <- new(GDALMultiDimRaster, vrt)
  arr <- ds$openMDArray("/t2m")

  # twice the resolution, bilinear
  arr2 <- arr$getResampled(c(NA, 10, 12), "bilinear", "", NULL)
  print(arr2$getDimensions()[, c("name", "size")])
  print(arr2$getCoordinateValues(2))

  # Antarctic polar stereographic, size computed by GDAL
  arr3 <- arr$getResampled(NULL, "nearest", "EPSG:3031", NULL)
  print(arr3$getDimensions()[, c("name", "size")])

  arr3$close()
  arr2$close()
  arr$close()
  ds$close()
}

## gridding scattered points (GDAL >= 3.7)
if (gdal_version_num() >= gdal_compute_version(3, 7, 0)) {
  # four points as a multidimensional VRT with inline values
  pts <- '<VRTDataset><Group name="/">
    <Dimension name="n" size="4"/>
    <Array name="x"><DataType>Float64</DataType><DimensionRef ref="n"/>
      <InlineValuesWithValueElement><Value>0</Value><Value>10</Value>
      <Value>0</Value><Value>10</Value></InlineValuesWithValueElement>
    </Array>
    <Array name="y"><DataType>Float64</DataType><DimensionRef ref="n"/>
      <InlineValuesWithValueElement><Value>0</Value><Value>0</Value>
      <Value>10</Value><Value>10</Value></InlineValuesWithValueElement>
    </Array>
    <Array name="v"><DataType>Float64</DataType><DimensionRef ref="n"/>
      <InlineValuesWithValueElement><Value>1</Value><Value>2</Value>
      <Value>3</Value><Value>4</Value></InlineValuesWithValueElement>
    </Array>
  </Group></VRTDataset>'

  ds <- new(GDALMultiDimRaster, pts)
  v <- ds$openMDArray("/v")
  x <- ds$openMDArray("/x")
  y <- ds$openMDArray("/y")
  g <- v$getGridded("invdist:power=2.0", x, y, "RESOLUTION=2.5")
  print(g$getDimensions()[, c("name", "size")])
  print(matrix(g$read(NULL, NULL, NULL), nrow = 5, byrow = TRUE))

  g$close()
  x$close()
  y$close()
  v$close()
  ds$close()
}
\dontshow{\}) # examplesIf}
